WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:08.010
Eighty million years ago… dinosaurs ruled
the surface of planet Earth.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:14.090
Yet in the blink of a cosmic eye, this kingdom
would fall to a mass extinction, and an entirely

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.850
unexpected order of animals would take their
place.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:23.460
If a similar calamity were to upend nature’s
order once again, what kinds of incredible

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.120
lifeforms might emerge?

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:32.250
Set eighty million years from now, the Sol’Kesh
Bestiary is a speculative biology project

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.320
by the brilliant artist and worldbuilder Terry
Maranda.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:42.150
Through naturalist-style illustrations, it
explores a world where humanity has long disappeared,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.180
and evolution has drastically transformed
the animals we know today.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.210
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
explore this reborn Earth.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:57.700
And as always, you can support the artist
on Patreon to see more of the project using

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.360
the links in the description.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:09.870
Now, let’s travel to the far future, and
discover the creatures of the Sol’Kesh Bestiary…

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:15.520
Eighty million years from now, the island
of Sol’Kesh is a landmass where the extremes

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.120
of the environment meet the extremes of evolution.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.869
Today, some of the most extreme ecosystems
are coral reefs.

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:30.009
In the era of Sol’Kesh, coral has adapted
to thrive in the inter-tidal zone, allowing

00:01:30.009 --> 00:01:34.130
familiar reef life to take to the land.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:40.079
One of the first pioneers of these semi-terrestrial
reefs is the tentacled Cerrophol.

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:45.610
This semi-aquatic cephalopod creeps from the
ocean as the tide falls, feeding on mollusks

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.229
and crustaceans left exposed to the sun.

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:54.149
Incredible as this might sound, we already
have some documentation of cephalopods moving

00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:56.649
upon the land in a limited capacity.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:02.520
And while the Cerrophol is technically more
closely related to modern squids than octopus,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:09.220
80 million years is plenty of time for such
a creature to emerge given the right conditions.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.440
Cerrophol tentacles have hardened into claw
like tips for dragging their body across the

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:19.940
land — and for cracking open the shells
of urchins and crabs so they can feast.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.850
For like their squid ancestors, Cerrophols
are carnivores...

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.580
But there are larger carnivores in these reborn
reefs.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.879
The Lancelet is a slithering terror that searches
for the telltale rhythm of prey with their

00:02:34.879 --> 00:02:36.660
sensitive antennae.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:42.970
Perhaps unexpectedly, these ambush predators
are the descendants of the lethargic sea slug.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:48.830
Yet in the era of Sol’Kesh, the Lancelets
can move with whip-like speed, able to launch

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:55.000
themselves at prey with deadly accuracy and
spear them with their forward-facing prongs.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:01.330
In the arms-race of evolution, these reefs
are a warzone.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:07.330
Surveying the battlefield with their solitary
orange eye, the Gharril is one particularly

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:08.880
alien creature.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.050
With four loping limbs, they stride across
the sand, feeding on anything the tide dredges

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.050
up.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:21.080
Remarkably, these slimy carnivores are the
descendants of mollusks, although they’ve

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:25.069
evolved fleshy limbs convergent with other
land animals.

00:03:25.069 --> 00:03:29.970
Their unique method of digestion produces
a pearl of sorts in their gullet made from

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.550
compressed bone.

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:36.470
When threatened by a fearsome predator, they
can expel this cannon-ball like pearl with

00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:37.799
devastating force...

00:03:37.799 --> 00:03:44.530
And few predators are as fearsome as the Daellash.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:49.760
These serpentine hunters live together upon
hatching, and when emerging from their burrows

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.409
resemble multi-headed hydras.

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:57.670
An offshoot of today’s garden eels, they’ve
retained their ancestor’s habit of peeking

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.900
out of the sand in unison.

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:05.920
With exceptionally large eyes, the Daellash
can track and snatch most any stray fish that

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:09.270
tumbles through the current with their trap-like
jaws.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:13.239
Yet these Daellash are only juveniles...

00:04:13.239 --> 00:04:16.799
Adult Daellash are large enough to hunt alone.

00:04:16.799 --> 00:04:23.480
These full-grown terrors are expert hunters,
able to brave a wide variety of environments.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:29.190
Like modern Moray Eels, they are experts at
navigating tight corners, and lashing out

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.220
from hidden caverns to ensnare prey.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:42.110
The Daellash are further proof that this new
reef environment is a realm of fearsome competition...

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:46.200
Moving inland, the shore gives way to a mosaic
of swampland.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:52.470
In our era, swamps and other wetlands only
cover around 7% of the Earth’s surface,

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:57.700
yet are home to numerous unique lifeforms
that have adapted to both land and water.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:05.090
And 80 million years in the future, many of
these animals look quite different…

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:11.050
Rising to the height of a large dog, the Solmundis
slinks through the wetlands hunting for prey.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:17.500
While this amphibian has become a feared ambush
predator in the shallow ponds of these swamps,

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.860
their ancestor is none other than the humble
lungless salamander.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:26.900
The Solmundis’s tongue is tipped with a
deadly bone spur that sits beneath their skull

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.440
while at rest.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:34.210
To hunt, the Solmundis lashes out with this
spear-like tongue to impale and quickly retract

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:40.390
their prey into a gluttonous, needle-toothed
maw.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:45.460
In the impenetrable murk of the waters that
flood the woodlands, the Murrow sense their

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.139
surroundings clearly.

00:05:47.139 --> 00:05:53.040
Though blind, these gargantuan fish use their
many sensitive whiskers in combination with

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:59.160
low-voltage electrical pulses to detect the
movement of prey — much like how their ancestors,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.250
the catfish, do today.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:07.680
Creatures outside the water are equally unsafe,
as this predator can quickly breach the opaque

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:13.750
surface with a vacuum-like gulp, with prey
instantly disappearing into the Murrow gullet

00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:18.750
as they sink back contently into the thick
silt.

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:24.560
Roaming in small herds across the tidal flats,
the Pebbleback Auhk show that some mammals

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.540
still persist in this strange future.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:32.220
While these sheep-like creatures might seem
out of place in a swampy environment, they

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:37.009
are well adapted for a lifestyle that’s
a bit soggier than what most mammals would

00:06:37.009 --> 00:06:38.349
put up with.

00:06:38.349 --> 00:06:44.180
Despite their appearance, the Auhk are actually
highly derived offshoots of the hippopotamus,

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.580
which are already semi-aquatic.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:52.250
To stay safe while wading into treacherous
waters, the Auhk possess a wide crest of horns

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:56.650
and rows of hardened osteoderms (awsteo-derms)
to deter predators.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:03.050
For nightmarish creatures lurk in the depths
of the swamp.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:08.230
In the sullen green light of the kelp forests,
the Larkiera slides through the tall plants

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:10.250
without making a whisper.

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:15.520
Large enough to feed on other predators like
the Murrow, the Larkiera is a descendant of

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:23.259
modern freshwater fish that has grown to a
startling size...

00:07:23.259 --> 00:07:28.319
North of the swampy coast, the land gives
way to great, untamed forests.

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:34.440
Today, the world has lost over one-third of
its forests due to human activity, but in

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:40.660
the time of Sol’Kesh, the forests have rebounded
in the extreme — allowing familiar woodland

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:45.720
animals to evolve into unfamiliar forms…

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:51.830
Tip-toeing unseen through the northern timberlands
are the shy and skittish Frondilli.

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:57.479
Covered in specialized waxy hairs that mimic
leaves, these camouflaged herbivores move

00:07:57.479 --> 00:07:59.910
with the utmost caution.

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:05.470
Unsurprisingly, the Frondilli are relatives
of modern deer, although a change in diet

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:08.420
has led to them becoming significantly smaller.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:13.669
They feed on the sap that runs through the
northern trees, breaking into the bark with

00:08:13.669 --> 00:08:16.190
their wedge-like, razored jaw.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:21.000
While feeding, the Frondilli keep an ear out
for potential danger, and at the slightest

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.090
snap of a twig they scurry back into hiding.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:32.019
High among the colossal conifers of mountainous
forests, the Calitross quietly climbs from

00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:33.599
branch to branch.

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:38.770
These avian creatures have long lost their
ability to fly, with their phalanges having

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:45.040
regressed back to a more digit like form — with
curved claws allowing for agile climbing.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:50.860
The Calitross are a close genetic descendant
of barn owls, and while they’ve lost their

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:56.080
ability to fly, in the branches the Calitross
have found a new niche as one of the most

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:01.200
versatile omnivores of the new world.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.980
While one lineage exits the skies, another
is just starting to stretch their wings.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:13.329
The Basilospri are a member of a reptilian
genus that is re-evolving powered flight — following

00:09:13.329 --> 00:09:17.010
in the footsteps of Pterosaurs millions of
years earlier.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:22.340
While still unsuited for long periods of flight,
the wingbeats of Basilospri might soon be

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:26.000
heard across the skies of Sol’Kesh.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:33.410
And speaking of making noise: a colossal omnivore
barges through the deep forest.

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:39.290
The Jhotus are so large they can topple small
trees with the heavy trod of their hooves,

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:43.720
and carve a path through leagues of woodland
in their endless pursuit of food.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:49.529
A supersized descendant of modern pigs, the
Jhotus have retained the boundless appetite

00:09:49.529 --> 00:09:51.480
of their ancestors.

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:56.750
This mighty genus sustains its size with a
slow metabolism, for there is little reason

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:03.680
for such a massive beast to hurry — especially
with their rock-hard hide and spiny fur.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:09.709
An immense network of cave systems winds through
the mountains of Sol’Kesh.

00:10:09.709 --> 00:10:15.920
In our era, the vast majority of underground
chasms remain unexplored — and in the depths

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:22.339
we have charted, the lifeforms within have
been much changed by their eons in isolation.

00:10:22.339 --> 00:10:27.959
Given 80 million more years, who knows what
might evolve…

00:10:27.959 --> 00:10:33.950
In the haunting glow of bioluminescent fungus,
a sightless terror clambers along the dripping

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.250
stalactites.

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:41.550
Molocks dwell in the darkness in large colonies,
navigating their maze-like home through echolocating

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:42.550
chirps.

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:48.550
A species of bat that adapted to a flightless
lifestyle while venturing ever deeper into

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:53.510
the tunnels, Molocks no longer have any need
to return to the surface.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:11:00.079
They are completely at home in the deep hydrogen-sulfide
based ecosystems that bloom far from the light

00:11:00.079 --> 00:11:06.260
— having long forgotten the warmth of the
sun.

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:11.950
Scuttling along the ceilings, walls, and floors
of the southeastern caves are the bizarre

00:11:11.950 --> 00:11:13.170
Mollucite.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:18.920
These well-camouflaged carnivores resemble
living stalagmites, and drag in prey with

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:21.570
their long, prehensile tongue.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:27.709
The Mollucite are a highly-derived offshoot
of scaly-foot gastropods, and like their ancestors

00:11:27.709 --> 00:11:32.670
can incorporate inorganic material into their
shells for protection.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:37.520
Yet the Mollucite have found another use for
the silica they extract from the cave tunnels

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:46.470
— having evolved a way to hone the substance
into sharp, obsidian like teeth…

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:52.220
In the tight confines of the limestone cavities
that riddle the western coastland, the sightless

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:55.860
Krainis live and die in utter darkness.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.700
These eel-like creatures can search for the
faintest pulse of heat from prey using highly

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:04.420
sensitive heat pits along the sides of their
head.

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:09.810
Having evolved from snakes that became accustomed
to life in the dark pits over millennia, the

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:13.630
Krainis haven’t lost the venom of their
predecessors.

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:19.670
To administer their venomous strike, the Krainis
possess forward facing fangs they use to spear

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:25.089
their prey in the narrow tunnels of the underground.

00:12:25.089 --> 00:12:30.230
In the deepest limestone caves, the enormous
Taurachni reigns supreme.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:35.519
A mighty arachnid, the Taurachni uses its
powerful front limbs to break through the

00:12:35.519 --> 00:12:38.589
tough shells of its preferred prey.

00:12:38.589 --> 00:12:44.220
While today the size of spiders is limited
by the level of oxygen in Earth’s atmosphere,

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:50.089
arthropods in the Sol’Kesh timeline have
evolved a more efficient means of respiration,

00:12:50.089 --> 00:12:55.260
allowing them to grow to sizes not seen since
the Carboniferous period.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:13:01.290
Extensive channels of spiracle openings provide
the Taurachni with extra oxygen, giving it

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:10.100
the fuel needed to support its thick exoskeleton
and armored limbs…

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:16.280
Back on the surface, a mighty, uninterrupted
grassland stretches across much of Sol’Kesh.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:21.820
In our era, grasslands are home to some of
the largest land animals on our planet.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:28.570
80 million years into the future, a new kingdom
of giants might emerge…

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:34.050
Slowly plodding across the flower fields of
western Sol’Kesh, the mighty Rutorts churn

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:37.420
up the earth in search of deep-soil tubers.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:43.149
Protected from predators by a thick external
shell, these herbivores are unlike any of

00:13:43.149 --> 00:13:46.110
the megafauna seen on the plains today.

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:52.030
For Rutorts are another distant relative of
arthropods, able to grow far larger than their

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:58.220
ancestors due to their highly efficient oxygen
intake and a more complex internal support

00:13:58.220 --> 00:13:59.220
structure.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:04.019
And thanks to their docile temperament, certain
birds have learned to nest on the backs of

00:14:04.019 --> 00:14:07.960
these mild-mannered giants.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:13.290
Amid the footsteps of behemoths, the quick
pitter-patter of taloned feet resonates over

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:17.190
the dry plains as the Axilot rushes past.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:23.450
These flightless avian creatures are scavengers
in a world of goliaths, digging into carcasses

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.470
with their sharp, wedge-shaped beaks.

00:14:26.470 --> 00:14:31.610
Today, numerous birds have traded in flight
for a life on the ground, although none are

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:33.579
as peculiar as the Axilot.

00:14:33.579 --> 00:14:39.740
Unlike most birds, the Axilot does not nurture
their young, instead leaving their eggs burrowed

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:40.899
in the soil.

00:14:40.899 --> 00:14:46.120
After a longer incubation than modern birds,
the fledglings are born with the ability to

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:52.490
run and hide from the titans of the plains…

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:57.980
One of the mightiest beasts dwells in the
rock-strewn dustbowl of the western plains.

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:04.089
The Grahknash are covered from snout to spiked
tail in thick armored plates, sharp enough

00:15:04.089 --> 00:15:08.130
to frighten all but the most ferocious of
predators.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:13.070
These standoffish creatures are descended
from one of the many spiny reptile lineages

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:15.570
that dwell in the deserts of today.

00:15:15.570 --> 00:15:21.079
And to take their armor to the next level,
the Grahknash are known to lick stones and

00:15:21.079 --> 00:15:26.200
mineral deposits to build up silica within
their bodies, further strengthening their

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.360
plating.

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:35.449
Wherever massive tunnels mar the surface of
the plains, a Korruk is surely nearby.

00:15:35.449 --> 00:15:41.329
Timid and almost apologetic in appearance,
these herbivores feed on deep-rooted plants

00:15:41.329 --> 00:15:44.280
that they chew with their large shearing teeth.

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:50.699
The Korruk have retained both the scaly armor-plating
and the spade-like claws of their pangolin

00:15:50.699 --> 00:15:51.699
ancestors.

00:15:51.699 --> 00:15:56.470
The claws of the Korruk aren’t just for
digging though, but also serve as an effective

00:15:56.470 --> 00:16:01.329
deterrent to mid-sized predators…

00:16:01.329 --> 00:16:04.580
Yet some predators aren’t deterred so easily.

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:10.319
With whooping howls, the monstrous Draffers
tear across the northern plains.

00:16:10.319 --> 00:16:15.970
These bipedal beasts show what happens when
a mammal converges on the predatory megafaunal

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:18.279
niche of the T-Rex.

00:16:18.279 --> 00:16:23.380
When a Draffer runs, their thick claws dig
into the cold earth for purchase, and their

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:29.250
powerful hind legs allow them to pursue prey
across the flat tundra with ease.

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:35.820
Few creatures on Sol’Kesh are as feared...

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:41.980
The final region of Sol’Kesh is also the
largest — the deep ocean.

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:46.960
Oceans currently cover over seventy percent
of our planet’s surface, and have given

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:50.569
rise to some of the most extraordinary species
on Earth.

00:16:50.569 --> 00:16:59.010
And in the era of the Sol’Kesh Bestiary,
ocean life has never been more spectacular…

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:06.049
Skimming over the waves in great flying shoals,
the Tiama feed on swarms of hovering insects.

00:17:06.049 --> 00:17:11.809
With their elongated mouths and keen eyesight,
these fish are expert bug-catchers.

00:17:11.809 --> 00:17:17.799
Perhaps unsurprisingly, the Tiama are related
to flying fish — although Tiama are far

00:17:17.799 --> 00:17:19.180
more aggressive.

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:25.550
If a shoal of these fish is sufficiently threatened,
the swarm becomes hostile, slashing predators

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:30.690
with their razor-sharp fins in consecutive
fly-bys.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:37.250
On the stony beaches of the Sol’Kesh coast,
the Daugon basks in the seaside sun.

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:42.580
These peculiar mammals possess large dorsal
sails to regulate their temperature, and to

00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:43.960
attract mates.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:49.300
The Daugon is another relative of the hippo,
having taken their semi-aquatic lifestyle

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:51.050
to new extremes.

00:17:51.050 --> 00:17:56.500
Their thick layers of blubber allow them to
survive the frigid depths of the sea floor,

00:17:56.500 --> 00:17:57.500
where they scavenge for large mollusks.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:03.150
A streamlined body makes for a quick descent
to the ocean floor to forage, a trip the Daugon

00:18:03.150 --> 00:18:09.140
must make daily to maintain their bulk.

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:14.720
One of the greatest monsters of all lurks
near the surface, their six eyes rising over

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:17.510
the choppy waves in search of prey.

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:23.610
The Arkiopti is a large crustacean that stealthily
treads water with their paddle-like limbs,

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:27.450
keeping their body hidden until it’s time
to strike.

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:33.539
Despite their impressive size, these creatures
are most closely related to current day shrimp.

00:18:33.539 --> 00:18:39.150
When prey lands close to the Arkiopti, they
release a mucus covered shard of stone at

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:46.690
their target, then drag their fresh meal below
the waves...

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:53.130
The Sol’Kesh Bestiary offers an inventive
interpretation of life in the far future.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:59.580
Yet in addition to being a speculative evolution
project, this bestiary is also available for

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:04.970
D&amp;D, with sheets for all creatures up on the
artist’s Patreon — as well as 3D printing

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:08.080
files made in collaboration with Edge Miniatures.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:13.710
Once again, there are links to follow and
support the artist Terry Maranda in the description

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:14.710
below.

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:18.309
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:19:18.309 --> 00:19:23.220
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:27.820
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:36.899
See you in the next video.

